BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
DTSTART:20261021T180000Z
DTEND:20261021T190000Z
SUMMARY:Keynote: Reclaiming the Commons: Restructuring Scholarly Communic
 ation with Digital Public Goods
DESCRIPTION:TopicCommercial publishing models continue to gatekeep schola
 rly outputs through high subscription costs and author fees. Drawing from
  Latin America's long-standing tradition of community-led openness\, coop
 eration\, and non-commercial knowledge sharing\, this keynote will explor
 e the necessary transformation of scholarly communication. By positioning
  open access infrastructure and non-commercial publishing models as essen
 tial digital public goods\, the session will reflect on regional models o
 f knowledge sovereignty and practical frameworks for building sustainable
 \, interoperable\, and ethically aligned open science infrastructures tha
 t return control of the academic commons to the global scholarly communit
 y.Keynote SpeakerArianna Becerril Garcíaholds a Ph.D. and M.Sc. in Compu
 ter Science from Tecnológico de Monterrey and a bachelor's degree in com
 puter engineering from Autonomous University of the State of Mexico (UAEM
 ex). She received the International Science Council's Early Career Scient
 ist Award and is a full-time professor and the Secretary of Science at UA
 EMex.She co-founded theRedalycopen infrastructure\, driving non-APC Diamo
 nd Open Access publishing across Latin America and now expanding worldwid
 e. She also co-founded and leadsAmeliCAOpen Knowledge\, a community-drive
 n initiative advocating non-commercial scholarly communication and region
 al knowledge sovereignty.Becerril García has served on advisory and coun
 cil boards for international organizations\, including the Directory of O
 pen Access Journals (DOAJ) and the National System of Scientific Publicat
 ions of Mexico. She collaborates on UNESCO-supported open science project
 s globally and coordinates a Latin American research group at the Latin A
 merican Council of Social Sciences (CLACSO).IntroductionAnita Say Chan\, 
 Chair and Professor\, Department of Information and Library Science\, Lud
 dy School of Informatics\, Computing\, and Engineering\, IU Bloomington
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p aria-level="2"><strong>Topic</strong><spa
 n data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134245418&quot\;:true\,&quot\;134245529&quot\;:
 true\,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:160\,&quot\;3355
 59739&quot\;:80\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><span data-c
 ontrast="auto">Commercial publishing models continue to gatekeep scholarl
 y outputs through high subscription costs and author fees. Drawing from L
 atin America's long-standing tradition of community-led openness\, cooper
 ation\, and non-commercial knowledge sharing\, this keynote will explore 
 the necessary transformation of scholarly communication. By positioning o
 pen access infrastructure and non-commercial publishing models as essenti
 al digital public goods\, the session will reflect on regional models of 
 knowledge sovereignty and practical frameworks for building sustainable\,
  interoperable\, and ethically aligned open science infrastructures that 
 return control of the academic commons to the global scholarly community.
 </span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740
 &quot\;:279}"></span></p><p aria-level="2"><strong>Keynote Speaker</stron
 g><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134245418&quot\;:true\,&quot\;134245529&q
 uot\;:true\,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:160\,&quot
 \;335559739&quot\;:80\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><a hre
 f="https://ciencia.uaemex.mx/en/about-us/organizational-chart-research/">
 <span data-contrast="none">Arianna Becerril García</span></a><span data-
 contrast="auto">holds a Ph.D. and M.Sc. in Computer Science from Tecnoló
 gico de Monterrey and a bachelor's degree in computer engineering from Au
 tonomous University of the State of Mexico (UAEMex). She received the Int
 ernational Science Council's Early Career Scientist Award and is a full-t
 ime professor and the Secretary of Science at UAEMex.</span><span data-cc
 p-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span
 ></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">She co-founded the</span><a href="http
 s://www.redalyc.org/"><span data-contrast="none">Redalyc</span></a><span 
 data-contrast="auto">open infrastructure\, driving non-APC Diamond Open A
 ccess publishing across Latin America and now expanding worldwide. She al
 so co-founded and leads</span><a href="https://amelica.org/index.php/en/h
 ome/"><span data-contrast="none">AmeliCA</span></a><span data-contrast="a
 uto">Open Knowledge\, a community-driven initiative advocating non-commer
 cial scholarly communication and regional knowledge sovereignty.</span><s
 pan data-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:2
 79}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">Becerril García has served
  on advisory and council boards for international organizations\, includi
 ng the Directory of Open Access Journals (DOAJ) and the National System o
 f Scientific Publications of Mexico. She collaborates on UNESCO-supported
  open science projects globally and coordinates a Latin American research
  group at the Latin American Council of Social Sciences (CLACSO).</span><
 span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:
 279}"></span></p><p aria-level="2"><strong>Introduction</strong><span dat
 a-ccp-props="{&quot\;134245418&quot\;:true\,&quot\;134245529&quot\;:true\
 ,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:160\,&quot\;335559739
 &quot\;:80\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><a href="https://
 luddy.iu.edu/people/chan-anita-say.html"><span data-contrast="none">Anita
  Say Chan</span></a><span data-contrast="auto">\, Chair and Professor\, D
 epartment of Information and Library Science\, Luddy School of Informatic
 s\, Computing\, and Engineering\, IU Bloomington</span><span data-ccp-pro
 ps="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p>
