BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
DTSTART:20261023T140000Z
DTEND:20261023T153000Z
SUMMARY:The Cost of Incentives: Making Open Scholarship the Norm
DESCRIPTION:But making open scholarship the norm raises difficult questio
 ns about incentives\, equity\, and institutional responsibility. How can 
 open scholarship be encouraged without it becoming another unsupported ex
 pectation\, particularly when researchers have unequal access to funding\
 , infrastructure\, time\, and disciplinary support? Should scholars be ex
 pected to adopt open practices\, and if so\, what responsibilities do uni
 versities\, publishers\, and other institutions have to make those practi
 ces feasible?  This panel will therefore examine efforts to normalize ope
 n scholarship through changes to research assessment\, scholarly communic
 ation\, and research infrastructure. It will ask not only how researchers
  might be encouraged to adopt open practices\, but also what universities
 \, publishers\, and other organizations must do to create the conditions 
 that make openness possible\, sustainable\, and professionally valued.Pan
 elistsMichael Dougherty (https://psyc.umd.edu/facultyprofile/dougherty/mi
 chael)\, Chair &amp\; Professor\, Department of Psychology\, University o
 f Maryland  Caitlin Schleicher (https://www.orcaopen.org/team)\, Director
 \, Community &amp\; Partnerships\, Open Research Community Accelerator  B
 odo Stern (https://www.hhmi.org/about/senior-leadership/bodo-stern)\, Chi
 ef of Strategic Initiatives\, Howard Hughes Medical Institute  ModeratorS
 cott Shackelford (https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/faculty-director
 y/profile.html?id=sjshacke)\, Associate Vice President for Research\, Ind
 iana University\; Vice Chancellor for Research\, IU Bloomington\; Provost
  Professor of Business Law &amp\; Ethics\, IU Bloomington\; Director of t
 he Program on Cybersecurity and Internet Governance\, Ostrom Workshop
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  But making open scholarship the norm ra
 ises difficult questions about incentives\, equity\, and institutional re
 sponsibility. How can open scholarship be encouraged without it becoming 
 another unsupported expectation\, particularly when researchers have uneq
 ual access to funding\, infrastructure\, time\, and disciplinary support?
  Should scholars be expected to adopt open practices\, and if so\, what r
 esponsibilities do universities\, publishers\, and other institutions hav
 e to make those practices feasible?</p><p>  This panel will therefore exa
 mine efforts to normalize open scholarship through changes to research as
 sessment\, scholarly communication\, and research infrastructure. It will
  ask not only how researchers might be encouraged to adopt open practices
 \, but also what universities\, publishers\, and other organizations must
  do to create the conditions that make openness possible\, sustainable\, 
 and professionally valued.</p><p><strong>Panelists</strong></p><ul><li><a
  href="https://psyc.umd.edu/facultyprofile/dougherty/michael">Michael Dou
 gherty</a>\, Chair &amp\; Professor\, Department of Psychology\, Universi
 ty of Maryland  </li><li><a href="https://www.orcaopen.org/team">Caitlin 
 Schleicher</a>\, Director\, Community &amp\; Partnerships\, Open Research
  Community Accelerator  </li><li><a href="https://www.hhmi.org/about/seni
 or-leadership/bodo-stern">Bodo Stern</a>\, Chief of Strategic Initiatives
 \, Howard Hughes Medical Institute  </li></ul><p><strong>Moderator</stron
 g></p><ul><li><a href="https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/faculty-dir
 ectory/profile.html?id=sjshacke">Scott Shackelford</a>\, Associate Vice P
 resident for Research\, Indiana University\; Vice Chancellor for Research
 \, IU Bloomington\; Provost Professor of Business Law &amp\; Ethics\, IU 
 Bloomington\; Director of the Program on Cybersecurity and Internet Gover
 nance\, Ostrom Workshop  </li></ul>
UID:20261023T140000Z-2426618@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260922T110920Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426618-the-cost-of-incentives-
 making-open-scholarship-the
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T154759Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop
 /1/src_region/0,0,3200,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.1789410307.j
 pg
